        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Poitiers (1356)</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">19 September 1356</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">On a wooded hill in the Forêt de Nouaillé, 5 miles (8 km) south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">Anglo-Gascons under Edward, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a>, generally considered by modern historians to number 6,000 (some contemporary accounts give 4,800 or 5,000); French under King John II, between 14,000 and 16,000</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The French were defeated and John II was captured with his youngest son Philip and between 2,000 and 3,000 men-at-arms. The Anglo-Gascons withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> with their prisoners.</span></div>
