    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Anglo-Gascons, about 6,000 by modern estimates, held a prepared position and beat a French army of between 14,000 and 16,000, almost all of whom fought on foot.</li><li>The battle was fought in four French attacks, and the last was broken when a small mounted force of 160 men sent round the flank appeared behind the French, who believed themselves surrounded and fled.</li><li>King John II was captured, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a> of 1360 ceded vast areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and ransomed him for three million gold écu, though the French resumed the war in 1369.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The French sacred banner, the Oriflamme, was unfurled in the last attack, which signalled that no prisoners were to be taken, yet the Anglo-Gascons were eager to take the French elite prisoner for ransom and many were captured.</p></aside>
    </section>

