    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1337</span><span class="rf-text">Philip VI's Great Council takes back the lands held by Edward III in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which begins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1355</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a> sets out on a chevauchée from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> and back</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1356</span><span class="rf-text">The Anglo-Gascon army marches north from Bergerac and devastates the countryside</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1356</span><span class="rf-text">The Anglo-Gascons rout part of the French rearguard near Savigny-Lévescault</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1356</span><span class="rf-text">The Anglo-Gascons repel three French attacks and win the fourth, and King John II is captured</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1357</span><span class="rf-text">A truce of two years is agreed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a> takes John to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1360</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a> cedes large areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and ransoms John</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1369</span><span class="rf-text">Large-scale fighting resumes</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-poitiers-1356" data-v="9382b430ce" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies take their ground</b> The Anglo-Gascons held a wooded hill, the Prince's division behind the other two as a reserve. The French drew up in four divisions, the first about 500 yards from the English.</li><li><b>The French cavalry charge the two wings</b> Audrehem's horsemen rode at Warwick's division and Clermont's at Salisbury's. On the left the marsh kept them from the archers. On the right they reached the gap in the hedge and a fierce melee began.</li><li><b>The first division is beaten and the second attacks</b> The cavalry were driven back with heavy losses and the first division was forced to withdraw under the English archers' fire. The second division, 4,000 strong, then attacked at the hedge, and the Prince committed his reserve.</li><li><b>The Dauphin's division withdraws, and Orléans leaves</b> After two hours the Dauphin's division withdrew in order. Orléans marched away with half of the third division. The rest made a feeble attack that was easily repelled, and some of Warwick's men left their line in pursuit.</li><li><b>The King's division advances; the Captal rides round</b> The King's division, swelled by survivors of the earlier attacks, marched across the gap with the Oriflamme unfurled. The Anglo-Gascons sent a small mounted force under the Captal de Buch round the French flank.</li><li><b>The Prince orders a general advance</b> The Black Prince ordered a general advance and the Anglo-Gascons moved out of their position. Warwick's men returning from the pursuit reinforced the line as the Captal's force worked round to the French rear.</li><li><b>The French are attacked from behind and break</b> The Captal's 160 men struck the French rear. The division fell apart, and those still fighting around the King were forced into a loop of the River Miosson. They were surrounded, and John and his youngest son surrendered.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince.jpg" alt="Edward the Black Prince" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Edward the Black Prince</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TombaPrincepNegre_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Josep Renalias, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of 1066 English monarchs had held lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which made them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the kings of France. By the early fourteenth century the only significant French possession left to them was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, which mattered out of proportion: the duty on wine from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> totalled more than all other English customs duties combined. On 24 May 1337 Philip VI's Great Council agreed that Edward III's lands in France should be taken back, on the grounds that he had broken his obligations as a vassal. This marked the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>.</p><p>Edward III could spare few resources for Gascony, whose people could usually field 3,000 to 6,000 men. Calais fell to the English in August 1347 and the Truce of Calais followed, partly because both countries were exhausted financially. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, estimated to have killed a third of the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, halted the fighting until 1350. A treaty negotiated at Guînes was signed on 6 April 1354, but John II, who succeeded Philip, decided not to ratify it after sentiment in his council turned against its terms, and from the summer of 1355 both sides were committed to full-scale war.</p><p>In 1355 Edward's eldest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Edward of Woodstock</a>, later known as the Black Prince, took the Gascon command and reached Bordeaux on 20 September with 2,200 English soldiers. Gascon nobles brought him to between 5,000 and 6,000. On 5 October he set out on a chevauchée, a large-scale mounted raid, as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> and back, returning on 2 December. No territory was captured, but enormous economic damage was done to France.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Poitiers&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Black Prince arrives, 1356, Manoeuvres:</p><ul><li>Jonathan Sumption, Trial by Fire (Faber and Faber, 1999)</li><li>Clifford J. Rogers, War Cruel and Sharp: English Strategy under Edward III, 1327–1360 (Boydell Press, 2014)</li><li>Peter Hoskins, In the Steps of the Black Prince: The Road to Poitiers, 1355–1356 (Boydell, 2011)</li><li>John A. Wagner, Chevauchée of 1355 (Greenwood, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Modern historians generally consider the Anglo-Gascon army to have numbered 6,000: 3,000 men-at-arms, 2,000 English and Welsh longbowmen and 1,000 Gascon infantry, many of them with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbows</a> or javelins. Some contemporary accounts give 4,800 or 5,000. All rode, but all or nearly all dismounted to fight. They stood in three divisions. Thomas, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick">Warwick</a>, held the left with about 1,000 men-at-arms and about 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>. William, Earl of Salisbury, held the right with the same. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a> commanded the centre of about 1,000 men-at-arms and 1,000 Gascon infantry, held back behind the others as a reserve. Each division was four to five men deep.</p><p>The French army numbered between 14,000 and 16,000: 10,000 to 12,000 men-at-arms, 2,000 crossbowmen and 2,000 other infantry. Almost all fought on foot, except two small groups of mounted knights, 300 or 500 in all, led by the marshals Arnoul d'Audrehem and Jean de Clermont. There were four divisions. The first, under the constable Walter, Count of Brienne, included 200 Scottish men-at-arms under William Douglas and most of the infantry and crossbowmen. The second, of 4,000 men-at-arms, was led by the Dauphin Charles, aged 19, and Peter, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>. The third, of about 3,200, was led by John's brother Philip, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>. John himself commanded the last, of 2,000 men-at-arms and an uncertain number of crossbowmen.</p><p>On 18 September the Anglo-Gascons took a position 5 miles south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> on a wooded hill in the Forêt de Nouaillé and prepared it for defence with pits, trenches and barricades. They were confident that on ground of their choosing they could defeat a larger French force. Their food was almost exhausted and they could not find enough water for their horses.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Poitiers&#8221; cites where it covers Anglo-Gascon army, French army:</p><ul><li>Clifford J. Rogers, War Cruel and Sharp: English Strategy under Edward III, 1327–1360 (Boydell Press, 2014)</li><li>Christopher Rothero, The Armies of Crécy and Poitiers (Osprey, 1995)</li><li>Peter Hoskins, In the Steps of the Black Prince: The Road to Poitiers, 1355–1356 (Boydell, 2011)</li><li>David Nicolle, Poitiers 1356: The Capture of a King (Osprey Publishing, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The French drew up about 500 yards from the English just after dawn. After about two hours they saw movement among the English and took it for a withdrawal. Modern scholars debate whether it was wagons moving to the rear, a staged withdrawal or a feint. The leaders of the first French division ordered an advance. Audrehem's cavalry attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick">Warwick</a>'s division, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> stood at the edge of a marsh that kept the horsemen from them, and they turned their fire on the crossbowmen. Audrehem was captured. On the right Clermont's cavalry reached a gap in a hedge and was forced back with heavy losses; Clermont and Brienne were killed.</p><p>The second French division, 4,000 strong, then attacked through the hedge, with hand-to-hand fighting for two hours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> was killed and the Dauphin's standard-bearer captured, and the Dauphin was withdrawn from the front. His division withdrew in good order. Half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>'s third division marched away, and three of John's four sons left the field; the rest made a feeble attack that was easily repelled.</p><p>John's fourth division, probably about 4,000 men-at-arms with crossbowmen, advanced on foot with the Oriflamme unfurled. The Anglo-Gascons, exhausted and short of arrows, sent about 160 mounted men under the Captal de Buch round the French flank, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a> ordered a general advance. The French slowly gained the upper hand until men of Warwick's division returned from pursuit and the Captal de Buch's force struck the French rear. Some Frenchmen ran, others followed and the division fell apart. Those around John were forced into a loop of the River Miosson and surrounded. John and his son Philip surrendered.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Poitiers&#8221; cites where it covers First attack, Second attack, Third attack, Fourth attack:</p><ul><li>Clifford J. Rogers, War Cruel and Sharp: English Strategy under Edward III, 1327–1360 (Boydell Press, 2014)</li><li>Peter Hoskins, In the Steps of the Black Prince: The Road to Poitiers, 1355–1356 (Boydell, 2011)</li><li>Christopher Rothero, The Armies of Crécy and Poitiers (Osprey, 1995)</li><li>Jonathan Sumption, Trial by Fire (Faber and Faber, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Frenchmen who fled soon after the Captal de Buch arrived mostly reached their horses. Many fled towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, whose citizens closed the gates and manned the walls, and the mounted Anglo-Gascons caught and killed them outside the gate. It was evening before the last Anglo-Gascons returned with their prisoners.</p><p>According to different modern sources 2,000 to 3,000 French men-at-arms and either 500 or 800 common soldiers were taken prisoner. They included the king, his youngest son, the archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a> and one of the two marshals. About 2,500 French men-at-arms were killed, with 3,300 common soldiers according to English accounts or 700 according to French ones. The dead included the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, the constable, the other marshal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Châlons and the standard-bearer Geoffroi de Charny. The Anglo-Gascons reported only 40 to 60 killed, of whom 4 were men-at-arms.</p><p>The Anglo-Gascons buried their dead and paroled some prisoners, then resumed their march on 21 September, slowly, laden with plunder. They entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libourne">Libourne</a> on 2 October, and about two weeks later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a> escorted John into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>. A truce of two years was agreed in March 1357. In April the Black Prince sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with John and landed at Plymouth on 5 May, and they were received rapturously in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Poitiers&#8221; cites where it covers Casualties, March to Bordeaux, Peace:</p><ul><li>Peter Hoskins, In the Steps of the Black Prince: The Road to Poitiers, 1355–1356 (Boydell, 2011)</li><li>Clifford J. Rogers, War Cruel and Sharp: English Strategy under Edward III, 1327–1360 (Boydell Press, 2014)</li><li>Jonathan Sumption, Trial by Fire (Faber and Faber, 1999)</li><li>Anne Curry, The Hundred Years' War 1337–1453 (Osprey Publishing, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Contemporary chroniclers on both sides recorded the battle. A French chronicler called the second attack more amazing, harder and more lethal than the others, and an English account described men fighting frenziedly. Their figures differ. Some contemporary accounts give the Anglo-Gascon army as 4,800 or 5,000 where modern historians say 6,000, and English accounts count 3,300 common French soldiers dead where French ones count 700. The Anglo-Gascon dead are reported as only 40 to 60, which Peter Hoskins calls improbably low.</p><p>Much of the detail rests on what these accounts leave open, and modern historians dispute it. The movement among the English that began the fighting has more than one explanation. The reasons for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>'s departure are contradictory: official French accounts that John ordered him to take the princes to safety were widely disbelieved and rumoured to have been invented. It is unclear whether the Anglo-Gascons pursued after the second attack. A chronicler's report that all the Anglo-Gascon men-at-arms remounted is generally discounted. Whether Audley led a charge at the king is uncertain, and the numbers in it run from 4 to 400.</p><p>Historians also differ on whether the French or the Anglo-Gascons had more men at the last stage, and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a>'s sincerity in the negotiations, since Edward III had given him leave to make a truce on his own authority. A computer analysis by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> University of Technology in 2017 found that heavy bodkin arrows could penetrate typical plate armour of the time at 250 yards.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war.jpg" alt="Hundred Years' War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_von_Azincourt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Clifford Rogers calls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a>'s chevauchée the most important campaign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> English and Gascon forces raided widely across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against little or no opposition, and with no effective central authority France dissolved into near anarchy. A peasant revolt, the jacquerie, broke out in northern France in the spring of 1358 and was bloodily put down in June.</p><p>Negotiations to free John were protracted. The First Treaty of London of May 1358 lapsed because the French government could not raise the first instalment of the ransom, and the Second Treaty of London was rejected by the Dauphin and the Estates General in May 1359. After a further campaign by Edward III in northern France, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a> was agreed by 8 May 1360. Vast areas of France were ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, to be ruled by the Black Prince, and John was ransomed for three million gold écu. Rogers states that Edward gained a full third of France in full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, his original war aims and much more. Sixteen senior nobles captured at Poitiers were released with it.</p><p>The peace seemed to end the war, but large-scale fighting broke out again in 1369. The war did not end until 1453, with a French victory that left only Calais in English hands.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> on 19 September 1356 about 6,000 Anglo-Gascons under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a> held a prepared position against a French army of between 14,000 and 16,000, and captured King John II. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a> followed in 1360, but the French resumed the war in 1369.</em></p>

